WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
How do you test what causes a disease, without experimenting on patients?

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.580
Watching patients shows what fails, not which broken part came first.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.920
To test cause directly, scientists recreate one disease feature inside an animal.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.820
They build that feature with genes, toxins, injury, or patient antibodies.

5
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.180
Deleting the muscle protein MuSK stops nerve-to-muscle junctions from forming in

6
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:36.580
newborn mice. Toxins kill specific brain neurons, and loud noise injures inner ear

7
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:37.580
synapses.

8
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:42.693
Inside a living animal, researchers can intervene, not only watch.

9
00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:48.313
Removing one link at a time in mouse muscle reveals each protein's job.

10
00:00:49.653 --> 00:00:54.533
An animal model recreates one disease feature to test cause, never a whole human.

11
00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:58.727
Now picture a mouse born without one synapse protein.

12
00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:04.787
Its gene is Mecp2, the mouse version of the gene mutated in Rett syndrome.

13
00:01:06.087 --> 00:01:09.507
By young adulthood it has symptoms, yet two histories fit.

14
00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:14.347
Either the circuit was built wrong early, or it simply cannot run.

15
00:01:15.747 --> 00:01:19.147
Switching that gene on or off late, which two tests separate them?

16
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:28.317
First, put the gene back after symptoms begin, and watch.

17
00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:33.097
Second, take it from a typical mouse after its circuit is built.

18
00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:39.357
If symptoms fade on return and appear on late loss, the protein runs the circuit.

19
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:42.477
Clean tests need sharp controls.

20
00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:47.057
A knockout deletes a whole protein, removing every job.

21
00:01:48.357 --> 00:01:52.117
A knock-in edits one site, keeping the protein to test one role.

22
00:01:53.537 --> 00:01:58.457
Measurements can mislead too: after loud noise, a mouse can pass its hearing test.

23
00:01:59.757 --> 00:02:03.657
Yet it can lose about half the ribbon synapses at one inner ear spot.

24
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:07.393
Even clean mouse results face species limits.

25
00:02:08.673 --> 00:02:13.213
One mouse strain, sex, age, and exposure cannot show what holds in people.

26
00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:19.573
The antibody cetuximab shrank mouse lung tumours driven by a mutant receptor.

27
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:25.733
Yet in patients, adding it to the drug afatinib as a first treatment did not help.

28
00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:31.253
Within those limits, animal models unlocked major causes and targets.

29
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:38.013
In 1975, Toyka and colleagues transferred antibodies from patients with

30
00:02:38.013 --> 00:02:40.753
muscle-weakening myasthenia gravis into mice.

31
00:02:42.093 --> 00:02:46.953
The mice lost muscle junction receptors and had smaller unit signals, called

32
00:02:46.953 --> 00:02:47.953
miniatures.

33
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:54.647
In 1990, Bergman, Wichmann, and DeLong tested a brain-circuit prediction in monkeys.

34
00:02:55.987 --> 00:03:02.107
They used the toxin MPTP to kill dopamine neurons and model Parkinson's disease.

35
00:03:03.627 --> 00:03:08.407
A lesion in the STN, a deep neuron cluster, eased opposite-side signs.

36
00:03:09.587 --> 00:03:12.487
That result made the STN a surgical target.

37
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.540
In 2017, Michalski and colleagues used a knock-in mouse to test the inner ear

38
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:20.760
protein otoferlin.

39
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.660
Changing one calcium-binding domain kept the protein present yet lowered the calcium

40
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:28.920
sensitivity of release.

41
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.460
Each model isolated one mechanism; human studies confirm what holds in patients.
